    {
      "ref": "PYQ-PHY-0ecc7bb0f8f1",
      "subtopic": "general",
      "type": "single_correct",
      "difficulty": 5,
      "expected_time_sec": 120,
      "source_type": "pyq",
      "exam": "jee_main",
      "year": 2026,
      "shift": "28 Jan 2026, Shift 2",
      "status": "draft",
      "stem": "As shown in the figure, a spring is kept in a stretched position with some extension by holding the masses 1 kg and 0.2 kg with a separation more than spring natural length and are released. Assuming the horizontal surface to be frictionless, the angular frequency (in SI unit) of the system is :",
      "image": {
        "url": "/question-images/pyq/physics/oscillations/PYQ-PHY-0ecc7bb0f8f1.png",
        "alt": "Figure for this Oscillations question"
      },
      "options": [
        {
          "label": "A",
          "content": "20",
          "is_correct": false
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "content": "5",
          "is_correct": false
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "content": "30",
          "is_correct": true
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "content": "27",
          "is_correct": false
        }
      ],
      "solution": "Key idea: two free masses joined by a spring oscillate about their centre of mass. This is like one body of reduced mass $\\mu$ on the same spring, so $\\omega = \\sqrt{k/\\mu}$.\nThe reduced mass is $\\mu = \\dfrac{m_1 m_2}{m_1+m_2} = \\dfrac{(1\\,\\text{kg})(0.2\\,\\text{kg})}{1.2\\,\\text{kg}} = \\dfrac{1}{6}\\,\\text{kg}$.\nSubstitute into the formula: $\\omega = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{150\\,\\text{N/m}}{(1/6)\\,\\text{kg}}} = \\sqrt{900}\\ \\text{rad/s}$.\nSo $\\omega = 30\\ \\text{rad/s}$.\nAnswer: (C) 30"
    },
